# 0019. `/fork`: branch the current thread

- Status: Accepted (owner, 2026-11-25; revised after Codex's critique the same day)
- Date: 2026-11-25

[0008](0008-slash-commands-and-mentions.adr) lists `/fork` ("branch the current thread
here") as later; [0010](0010-filesystem-event-log.adr) already reserves `parent_id` and
`fork_event` in `meta.json`. This ADR decides what a fork copies and where it can branch.

## Technical details
- **`/fork [title]`**: a new thread that starts with everything the current one has, and
  goes its own way from here. The old thread stays as it was and can be resumed.
- **Branch point: the end of the thread only** (v1). Both CLIs fork a session as a whole
  (Claude `--resume <id> --fork-session`, Codex `codex exec fork <id>` /
  `codex fork`); neither can branch at an earlier message, and the 0003 rule still holds:
  ship only what both adapters can do natively. Forking from a selected earlier line is
  a later extension (it would need a summary seeded into fresh sessions, see
  [0020](0020-handoff.adr)).
- **Refused while busy**: a fork with a reply running or queued says "wait for replies to
  finish, or Esc", because a half-finished turn can't be in both threads.
- **Files**:
  - new `threads/<ulid>/meta.json`: `parent_id` = old thread id, `fork_event` = the old
    thread's last `seq`, `title` = given or `<old title> (fork)`, same `checkout`;
  - `events.jsonl` starts with one `status` line `forked from <id>@<seq>`; parent events
    are not copied.
  - **One ancestry reader** serves `/resume`, `/log`, history builders and `/context`: it
    reads the parent's events up to `fork_event`, then the child's own (recursively for
    forks of forks). Every event read this way is addressed as `(thread_id, seq)`, and
    its `turn`/`parent` resolve within its own thread, so equal `seq` numbers in parent
    and child never collide. A missing parent, a `fork_event` past the parent's end, or
    a cycle in `parent_id` fails visibly ("cannot open fork: …"), never a partial thread.
  - **Inherited is conversation only**: messages, tool lines and handoffs. Approval grants
    ([0018](0018-approvals-in-the-chat.adr)), registry notes and usage stay with the thread
    that made them; a fork starts with no grants and counts only its own tokens.
  - **Native sessions are pinned at the fork point**, because the parent's session keeps
    moving if the parent is resumed and used before the child's first turn:
    - Claude: `/fork` stores the parent session id and the id of its last message; the
      child's first turn runs `--resume <parent> --resume-session-at <message id>
      --fork-session` (the flag works in 2.1.332 but is missing from `--help`).
    - Codex: `/fork` runs `codex exec fork <thread id>` at once, without a prompt, and
      stores the new thread id; the child resumes that.
    - Both boundaries are verified by a recorded fixture where the parent advances after
      `/fork` and before the child's first turn; an adapter whose boundary cannot be
      pinned refuses `/fork` for its sessions with that reason.
    Parent and child never share a native session.
  - free models: their history is rebuilt from the inherited events, so nothing extra.
- **Usage**: tokens of the shared part stay counted in the parent; the fork's
  `usage.json` starts empty and `/usage thread` on a fork counts only its own.
- **Threads pane**: a fork shows under its parent, indented (`↳ title`).
- **Writer lock**: unchanged; forks share the checkout, so one writer at a time across
  both ([0012](0012-checkout-writer-lock.adr)).
- Consequences: try two directions from the same point without losing either. Costs: a
  thread's history can now span folders, so readers (`/resume`, `/log`, history builders)
  follow `parent_id`.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: claude `--fork-session` and `--resume-session-at` (2.1.332), codex `exec fork` (0.172.0)
- Alternatives:
  - Copying the parent's events into the fork: self-contained folders, but doubles storage
    and breaks "append-only, one place" for usage.
  - Fork at any line now, CLI agents restarted with a summary: works everywhere, but the
    agents lose their real context; better as an explicit later option.
